    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The \"efficiency\" argument seems pretty standard, but I've been seeing a lot of pushback on that lately. Some people say that even if prices are low now, the long-term damage to the actual market structure is irreversible. \n\nIn your view, do these investigations actually have a shot at fixing things, or is the regulatory approach just fundamentally outdated for how the digital economy works?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This is a central tension in modern antitrust policy. Some argue that current, often 20th-century, rules designed for a physical economy are ill-equipped to handle data monopolies and network effects in the digital realm. However, advocates believe targeted regulation can force structural changes, such as interoperability or data portability, to foster healthier competition moving forward. The success hinges on whether regulators can move beyond price concerns to address market power itself.",
      "source": "target",
