    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a fair summary, but it feels like there's a pretty massive gap between those two sides. If you look at the actual outcomes people are predicting, it seems like one side is basically arguing for breaking up these companies to save the market, while the other is essentially saying government intervention will just kill the next big thing. \n\nIf you had to weigh them, is there a sense that the \"outdated rules\" argument is a legitimate critique, or is it more of a convenient shield used by these companies to avoid accountability?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"outdated rules\" critique is widely seen by many legal and tech experts as a legitimate and necessary challenge to the status quo. Proponents of this view argue that focusing solely on consumer price is an insufficient framework when the real issue is the control over essential digital infrastructure and data. While some corporate defense is certainly present, the innovative nature of these platforms genuinely requires a modern regulatory approach that can address non-price harms like innovation foreclosure, not just breakups.",
      "source": "target",
